Summary: PaymentsDisabledBootGuard (`PaymentsDisabledBootGuard.java:45-57`) is a `@PostConstruct` fail-fast check active only when `app.payments.enabled=false`; it throws `IllegalStateException` if any `CompanySubscription` row is in one of six in-flight PAID statuses (`TRIAL_ENTERPRISE`, `BUSINESS_ACTIVE`, `ENTERPRISE_ACTIVE`, `DOWNGRADE_PENDING`, `PAYMENT_FAILED`, `TERMS_PENDING`), deliberately excluding terminal `ACCOUNT_DEACTIVATED`/`SUSPENDED_LEGAL` rows so routine GDPR deletions can't brick startup. No production code calls it besides Spring's container lifecycle — moving it to a separate `ApplicationListener` would only require updating its own unit test. All three answers verified clean against the checkout; one correct abstention on line-level test detail (graph doesn't index source text), which I resolved myself by reading the file — found the terminal-status exclusion covered at `PaymentsDisabledBootGuardUnitTest.java:50-70`.
